/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/cwna.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("cwna", [["cw1",0,1,"An engineer reads that an access point is transmitting at 100 mW. What is this power level in dBm?",["10 dBm","20 dBm","30 dBm","100 dBm"],1,"10 × log10(100) = 20, so 100 mW is 20 dBm. 10 dBm would be 10 mW and 30 dBm would be 1,000 mW.","Domain 1 – RF math",["10 dBm is 10 mW; 100 mW is ten times more power, which adds another 10 dB.",null,"30 dBm is 1,000 mW (1 W), ten times more than 100 mW.","100 dBm confuses the mW number with dBm; it would be 10^10 mW, far beyond any Wi-Fi radio."],2],["cw2",0,1,"A radio outputs 17 dBm through a cable with 2 dB of loss to an antenna rated at 6 dBi. What is the EIRP?",["23 dBm","25 dBm","19 dBm","21 dBm"],3,"EIRP = 17 − 2 + 6 = 21 dBm. Adding the cable loss instead of subtracting it gives the tempting 25 dBm.","Domain 1 – EIRP and link budget",["23 dBm ignores the 2 dB of cable loss and simply adds output power and antenna gain.","25 dBm adds the 2 dB cable loss instead of subtracting it; losses always reduce the power reaching the antenna.","19 dBm subtracts the cable loss but forgets to add the 6 dBi antenna gain, which increases the radiated power.",null],2],["cw3",0,1,"A laptop reports a received signal of −65 dBm and a noise floor of −92 dBm. What is the SNR?",["27 dB","157 dB","−27 dB","92 dB"],0,"SNR is signal minus noise: −65 − (−92) = 27 dB. Adding the absolute values gives the wrong 157.","Domain 1 – Signal metrics",[null,"157 dB adds the absolute values of signal and noise; SNR is the difference between them, not the sum.","A negative SNR would mean noise is stronger than signal; here the signal at -65 dBm is 27 dB above the -92 dBm noise floor.","92 dB is just the noise floor value without its sign; SNR must compare the signal against that noise."],1],["cw4",0,1,"A 40 mW transmitter has 3 dB of gain added to its output. About how much power results?",["120 mW","43 mW","80 mW","400 mW"],2,"Every 3 dB of gain roughly doubles power, so 40 mW becomes about 80 mW. 10 dB of gain would give 400 mW.","Domain 1 – RF math",["120 mW would be tripling the power; 3 dB of gain roughly doubles power rather than adding a factor of three.","43 mW adds the 3 dB value directly to the milliwatt figure; decibels are ratios, not milliwatts to add.",null,"400 mW is a tenfold increase, which is what 10 dB of gain gives, not 3 dB."],2],["cw5",0,1,"Which statement about 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz signals is correct?",["Both bands share one wavelength","2.4 GHz has the shorter wavelength","5 GHz has the shorter wavelength","Wavelength depends on transmit power"],2,"Wavelength is inversely related to frequency, so the higher 5 GHz band has the shorter wavelength (about 6 cm vs 12.5 cm). Power changes amplitude, not wavelength.","Domain 1 – RF characteristics",["Wavelength depends on frequency, so two bands at different frequencies cannot share one wavelength.","It is the reverse: 2.4 GHz is the lower frequency, so it has the longer wavelength (about 12.5 cm).",null,"Transmit power changes the amplitude of the wave, not its wavelength, which is fixed by frequency."],1],["cw6",0,1,"In a warehouse full of metal racking, a client receives several delayed copies of the same transmission. Which RF behavior mainly causes this?",["Reflection","Absorption","Refraction","Free space path loss"],0,"Metal surfaces reflect RF, creating multiple paths that arrive at different times (multipath). Absorption weakens a signal but does not create copies.","Domain 1 – RF behaviors",[null,"Absorption converts RF energy into heat and weakens the signal, but it does not produce delayed copies of it.","Refraction bends a wave as it passes between media of different density; it does not explain many echoes off metal racks.","Free space path loss is the weakening from the wave spreading out over distance and does not create multiple copies."],2],["cw7",0,1,"Signal in a lecture hall is strong when it is empty but drops sharply once it fills with students. Which behavior explains this?",["Scattering","Diffraction","Reflection","Absorption"],3,"The human body is mostly water, which absorbs RF energy and converts it to heat. Diffraction is bending around edges, not loss inside a material.","Domain 1 – RF behaviors",["Scattering happens off rough or uneven surfaces and small objects; it does not explain the energy lost into a crowd of water-filled bodies.","Diffraction is bending around the edge of an obstacle; the loss here happens inside the people themselves.","Reflection bounces signals off smooth surfaces such as metal or glass; human bodies mostly absorb 2.4 and 5 GHz energy rather than reflect it.",null],2],["cw8",0,1,"Users just behind the corner of a thick concrete wall still get a weak signal even though the wall blocks the direct path. Which behavior lets the signal reach them?",["Absorption in the wall","Diffraction around the wall's edge","Refraction through the air","Polarization of the signal"],1,"Diffraction bends a wave around the edge of an obstacle, leaving a weaker RF shadow behind it. Absorption only removes energy.","Domain 1 – RF behaviors",["Absorption only removes energy inside the wall; it cannot redirect the signal around the corner to the users.",null,"Refraction is bending as a wave passes through media of different density, such as air layers or glass, not bending around a wall edge.","Polarization is the orientation of the electric field; it does not let a signal travel around an obstruction."],2],["cw9",0,1,"An outdoor bridge with a completely clear path still shows lower received signal as the distance is increased. What is the main reason?",["The cable VSWR rises with distance","Water vapor absorbs most 5 GHz energy","Multipath always cancels the main signal","The wavefront spreads out with distance"],3,"Free space path loss comes from the wave spreading over a larger area as it travels, even with no obstacles. Weather effects are minor at these distances and VSWR does not depend on link length.","Domain 1 – RF behaviors",["VSWR reflects impedance mismatch in the cable and connectors, which does not change as the link gets longer.","Water vapor causes only minor attenuation at 5 GHz over typical bridge distances; it is not the main cause of this loss.","Multipath can cause fading, but it does not always cancel the signal and a clear outdoor path has little of it; it does not explain a steady drop with distance.",null],2],["cw10",0,1,"A point-to-point link was installed with one Yagi mounted vertically and the other horizontally. What is the likely result?",["Higher gain from polarization diversity","Heavy loss from polarization mismatch","No effect, since Yagis are omnidirectional","A wider beamwidth on both antennas"],1,"Antennas on a link should share the same polarization; a 90-degree mismatch causes large signal loss. Yagis are semi-directional, not omnidirectional.","Domain 1 – Antennas",["Polarization diversity is a deliberate multi-antenna design; mismatching the two ends of a link loses signal rather than adding gain.",null,"Yagis are semi-directional antennas, so the premise is wrong and polarization still matters.","Rotating an antenna changes its polarization, not its beamwidth; the beam pattern is unchanged but the link suffers mismatch loss."],2],["cw11",0,1,"Two buildings can see each other's rooftop antennas, but trees intrude on the area just below the visual path and the link is unstable. What should the engineer address?",["The ground plane under the mast","The antennas' back lobe","Clearance of the Fresnel zone","The horizontal beamwidth only"],2,"RF line of sight needs most of the first Fresnel zone clear, not just a visual path. Obstructions in it reduce and destabilize the signal.","Domain 1 – Point-to-point links",["A ground plane affects some antenna types' patterns, but the problem here is trees intruding into the path near the line of sight.","The back lobe is energy radiated behind the antenna; it does not explain an obstruction between the two buildings.",null,"Horizontal beamwidth describes antenna coverage width; the trees are blocking the Fresnel zone, which beamwidth changes will not fix."],3],["cw12",0,1,"A company needs a high-gain, narrow-beam link between two sites several kilometers apart. Which antenna type fits best?",["Parabolic dish","Omnidirectional dipole","Indoor ceiling patch","Wide-angle sector"],0,"Highly directional parabolic dishes provide the most gain and narrowest beam for long point-to-point links. Omnis and sectors spread energy over wide areas.","Domain 1 – Antennas",[null,"An omnidirectional dipole spreads energy all around with low gain, which is poor for a multi-kilometer point-to-point link.","An indoor ceiling patch is meant for indoor coverage and has modest gain; it is not built for long outdoor links.","A sector antenna covers a wide arc for point-to-multipoint service, so its gain and beam are too broad for a long single link."],1],["cw13",0,1,"An antenna datasheet lists a gain of 5 dBd. What is the equivalent gain in dBi?",["2.86 dBi","7.14 dBi","5 dBi","10 dBi"],1,"A dipole has 2.14 dBi of gain, so dBi = dBd + 2.14 = 7.14 dBi. Subtracting instead gives 2.86.","Domain 1 – RF math",["2.86 dBi subtracts the 2.14 dB dipole gain; converting dBd to dBi means adding it because dBi uses a lower reference.",null,"5 dBi would treat dBd and dBi as the same unit, ignoring the 2.14 dB gain of the dipole reference.","10 dBi doubles the number instead of adding the fixed 2.14 dB offset between the two references."],2],["cw14",0,1,"An AP radio is described as 3x3:2. What does this mean?",["3 spatial streams and 2 receive chains","3 streams, 3 antennas, 2 bands","3 radios per band and 2 bands in total","3 transmit chains, 3 receive chains, 2 streams"],3,"MIMO notation is transmit chains × receive chains : spatial streams. The radio can send two streams while using all three chains for diversity.","Domain 1 – MIMO",["MIMO notation lists transmit chains first, then receive chains, then streams, so 3x3:2 has two spatial streams, not three.","The notation says nothing about bands or antenna count; the number after the colon is spatial streams.","3x3:2 describes radio chains and streams in one radio, not the number of radios or bands.",null],1],["cw15",0,2,"Which organization writes and publishes the 802.11 standard and its amendments?",["IEEE","Wi-Fi Alliance","IETF","FCC"],0,"The IEEE maintains 802.11. The Wi-Fi Alliance certifies interoperability, the IETF writes internet protocols such as RADIUS, and the FCC regulates spectrum in the US.","Domain 2 – Standards bodies",[null,"The Wi-Fi Alliance certifies interoperability and names generations, but it does not write the 802.11 standard.","The IETF writes internet protocols such as RADIUS and IP, not the 802.11 wireless LAN standard.","The FCC regulates spectrum use in the United States; it does not author technical standards like 802.11."],1],["cw16",0,2,"A buyer wants assurance that a new client adapter will work with WPA3 on other vendors' access points. What should they look for?",["FCC equipment authorization for the adapter","IEEE 802.11 ratification of the adapter","Wi-Fi CERTIFIED WPA3 from the Wi-Fi Alliance","An IETF RFC number on the product box"],2,"The Wi-Fi Alliance tests products for interoperability and security features such as WPA3. FCC authorization covers emissions, not interoperability.","Domain 2 – Standards bodies",["FCC equipment authorization covers emissions and legal operation, not whether WPA3 interoperates with other vendors.","The IEEE ratifies standards, not individual products, and it does not test interoperability.",null,"IETF RFCs define internet protocols; they are not product certifications and say nothing about WPA3 interoperability."],2],["cw17",0,2,"Who decides which channels and maximum transmit power an AP may legally use in a given country?",["The Wi-Fi Alliance","The IEEE 802.11 working group","That country's regulatory authority","The AP manufacturer"],2,"National regulators, such as the FCC in the United States, set legal frequencies and power limits. The IEEE defines how the protocol works within those rules.","Domain 2 – Regulations",["The Wi-Fi Alliance certifies products but has no legal authority over channels or power in any country.","The IEEE 802.11 working group defines how the protocol works, but each country's regulator decides what is legal to use.",null,"The manufacturer must build products to meet local rules, but it does not set those rules."],1],["cw18",0,2,"An engineer is planning 20 MHz 2.4 GHz channels for an office in North America. Which set avoids overlap?",["1, 6 and 11","1, 5 and 9","2, 7 and 12","1, 4, 8 and 11"],0,"2.4 GHz channels are 5 MHz apart but about 20 MHz wide, so 1, 6 and 11 are the standard non-overlapping set in North America.","Domain 2 – Channels",[null,"Channels 1, 5 and 9 are only 20 MHz apart in center frequency, so their roughly 22 MHz-wide signals still overlap slightly and it is not the standard plan.","2, 7 and 12 has the right spacing, but channel 12 is not usable at normal power in North America, so it is not the standard plan there.","Four channels at 1, 4, 8 and 11 are only three channels apart, so adjacent ones overlap heavily."],1],["cw19",0,2,"Which 802.11 amendment introduced OFDMA and BSS coloring?",["802.11g","802.11ac","802.11n","802.11ax"],3,"802.11ax (HE, Wi-Fi 6) added OFDMA, BSS coloring, Target Wake Time and 1024-QAM. 802.11ac introduced downlink MU-MIMO but not OFDMA.","Domain 2 – 802.11ax",["802.11g brought OFDM to 2.4 GHz at up to 54 Mbps; it has no OFDMA or BSS coloring.","802.11ac added wider channels, 256-QAM and downlink MU-MIMO, but OFDMA and BSS coloring came later with 802.11ax.","802.11n introduced MIMO and 40 MHz channels, not OFDMA or BSS coloring.",null],1],["cw20",0,2,"A client's datasheet lists only VHT (802.11ac) rates. In which band can it use those rates?",["2.4 GHz only","5 GHz only","2.4 and 5 GHz","6 GHz only"],1,"802.11ac VHT is defined for 5 GHz only. 802.11ax (HE) works in 2.4, 5 and 6 GHz, which is the tempting mix-up.","Domain 2 – PHYs",["VHT is not defined for 2.4 GHz; a 2.4 GHz-only device would use HT or older rates.",null,"802.11ac VHT rates are 5 GHz only; in 2.4 GHz the client falls back to HT (802.11n) or older rates.","6 GHz Wi-Fi uses HE (802.11ax) or newer; VHT clients cannot operate in 6 GHz at all."],2],["cw21",0,2,"Which amendment first introduced MIMO and 40 MHz channels under the name HT?",["802.11ac","802.11a","802.11g","802.11n"],3,"802.11n (High Throughput, Wi-Fi 4) added MIMO and 40 MHz channels. 802.11ac later added 80 and 160 MHz.","Domain 2 – PHYs",["802.11ac (VHT) extended MIMO with 80/160 MHz channels and MU-MIMO; it did not introduce HT.","802.11a introduced OFDM in 5 GHz with a single stream and 20 MHz channels, with no MIMO.","802.11g brought ERP-OFDM to 2.4 GHz with single-stream 20 MHz channels, not MIMO.",null],1],["cw22",0,2,"An old barcode scanner supports only 1, 2, 5.5 and 11 Mbps. Which PHY does it use?",["ERP-OFDM (802.11g)","HR/DSSS (802.11b)","OFDM (802.11a)","HT (802.11n)"],1,"Those four rates are the DSSS and HR/DSSS rates of 802.11b. ERP-OFDM and OFDM rates start at 6 Mbps.","Domain 2 – PHYs",["ERP-OFDM (802.11g) uses OFDM rates from 6 to 54 Mbps; 1-11 Mbps only would indicate DSSS/HR-DSSS.",null,"802.11a OFDM runs at 6-54 Mbps in 5 GHz and never uses 1, 2, 5.5 or 11 Mbps.","HT (802.11n) uses MCS-based rates starting at 6.5 Mbps; a device limited to 1-11 Mbps is 802.11b."],2],["cw23",0,2,"An admin wants to add a 6 GHz radio to an existing WPA2-Personal SSID. What is the problem?",["6 GHz needs 802.11b protection","6 GHz allows only 20 MHz channels","6 GHz needs WPA3 or Enhanced Open","6 GHz cannot use any passphrase"],2,"Wi-Fi 6E rules require WPA3 (SAE or Enterprise) or OWE with PMF in 6 GHz; WPA2 is not allowed. 6 GHz supports wide channels and WPA3-Personal uses a passphrase.","Domain 2 – 6 GHz",["802.11b does not exist in 6 GHz and no protection mechanism for it is needed there.","6 GHz supports 20, 40, 80 and 160 MHz (and 320 MHz with Wi-Fi 7), so channel width is not the issue.",null,"WPA3-Personal (SAE) still uses a passphrase in 6 GHz; only WPA2-Personal's PSK method is disallowed."],2],["cw24",0,2,"An AP on 5 GHz channel 100 suddenly changes channel and clients disconnect briefly. The log shows a radar event. Which requirement caused this?",["Dynamic Frequency Selection","Transmit Power Control","Clear Channel Assessment","Automatic rate selection"],0,"DFS requires devices in radar-shared channels to leave when radar is detected. TPC controls power levels, not channel changes after radar.","Domain 2 – DFS and TPC",[null,"TPC lowers transmit power to reduce interference; it does not force a channel change after radar detection.","CCA is how a station senses whether the medium is busy before transmitting; it is not a regulatory radar requirement.","Rate selection changes data rates based on signal quality, not the channel after a radar event."],2],["cw25",0,2,"Why do clients at the edge of an 802.11ax cell never reach 1024-QAM data rates?",["1024-QAM works only on 2.4 GHz","1024-QAM needs a very high SNR near the AP","1024-QAM is limited to legacy clients","1024-QAM is disabled whenever DFS is on"],1,"Denser modulation packs more bits per symbol but needs a clean, strong signal. Far from the AP, SNR drops and the rate shifts to simpler modulation.","Domain 2 – 802.11ax",["1024-QAM is part of 802.11ax and can be used in 2.4, 5 and 6 GHz, so band is not the reason.",null,"Legacy clients cannot use 1024-QAM at all; it requires 802.11ax (HE) capable devices.","DFS governs radar detection on certain 5 GHz channels and does not disable any modulation type."],2],["cw26",0,2,"A dense office wants 40 MHz channels on 2.4 GHz for more speed. What is the best advice?",["Avoid it: 40 MHz exists only in 6 GHz","Use it: 2.4 GHz has four clean 40 MHz channels","Use it: bonding removes co-channel contention","Avoid it: only one clean 40 MHz channel fits"],3,"The 2.4 GHz band is too narrow for more than one non-overlapping 40 MHz channel, so bonding there causes heavy overlap. 40 MHz is common in 5 GHz.","Domain 2 – Channels",["40 MHz channels were introduced by 802.11n and are widely used in 5 GHz; they are not limited to 6 GHz.","The 2.4 GHz band is only about 83.5 MHz wide, so it cannot hold four clean 40 MHz channels.","Channel bonding makes each channel wider and leaves fewer non-overlapping channels, which increases co-channel contention.",null],3],["cw27",0,2,"Which amendment defines fast BSS transition so 802.1X clients can roam without a full reauthentication?",["802.11r","802.11k","802.11v","802.11w"],0,"802.11r (FT) pre-derives keys for the target AP. 802.11k gives neighbor reports and 802.11w protects management frames.","Domain 2 – Amendments",[null,"802.11k provides radio measurements and neighbor reports to help pick a target AP, but it does not speed up the key exchange.","802.11v provides BSS transition management and other network hints; it does not define fast key derivation for roaming.","802.11w protects management frames such as deauthentication; it has nothing to do with fast roaming."],1],["cw28",0,2,"A client asks its AP for a list of nearby APs to speed up roaming. Which amendment provides this neighbor report?",["802.11w","802.11r","802.11k","802.11e"],2,"802.11k radio resource measurement includes neighbor reports. 802.11r speeds up the key exchange once a target is chosen.","Domain 2 – Amendments",["802.11w defines protected management frames, not neighbor reports.","802.11r speeds up the authentication and key exchange during a roam, but it does not provide the list of nearby APs.",null,"802.11e defines QoS (EDCA), which prioritizes traffic classes rather than reporting neighboring APs."],1],["cw29",0,2,"A spec sheet says the AP supports Wi-Fi 5. Which amendment is that?",["802.11n","802.11ax","802.11ac","802.11be"],2,"The Wi-Fi Alliance names are Wi-Fi 4 (n), Wi-Fi 5 (ac), Wi-Fi 6/6E (ax) and Wi-Fi 7 (be).","Domain 2 – Generation names",["802.11n is Wi-Fi 4 under the Wi-Fi Alliance generation names.","802.11ax is Wi-Fi 6 (and Wi-Fi 6E in the 6 GHz band).",null,"802.11be is Wi-Fi 7, two generations after Wi-Fi 5."],1],["cw30",0,2,"Which list shows adjacent, non-overlapping 20 MHz channels in the 5 GHz U-NII-1 band?",["36, 40, 44, 48","1, 6, 11, 14","36, 38, 40, 42","100, 101, 102, 103"],0,"5 GHz channel numbers are 5 MHz apart, so 20 MHz channels step by four: 36, 40, 44 and 48 in U-NII-1.","Domain 2 – Channels",[null,"1, 6, 11 and 14 are 2.4 GHz channels, and 14 is only allowed in Japan for 802.11b; none are in U-NII-1.","Channels 38 and 42 are the center numbers used for 40 and 80 MHz bonded channels, so this list mixes widths and overlaps.","5 GHz channel numbers are 5 MHz apart, so 100, 101, 102 and 103 would overlap almost entirely and are not valid 20 MHz channels."],1],["cw31",0,2,"What does OFDMA let an 802.11ax AP do?",["Transmit on 2.4 GHz channel 14 in all regions","Bond two separate bands into one wide channel","Encrypt frames without any 4-way handshake","Serve several clients at once using resource units"],3,"OFDMA divides a channel into resource units so several clients can be served in one transmission, cutting overhead for small frames.","Domain 2 – 802.11ax",["Channel 14 is permitted only in Japan and only for 802.11b; OFDMA does not change regional channel rules.","Combining separate bands is not OFDMA; it relates to multi-link operation in Wi-Fi 7, while OFDMA subdivides one channel.","OFDMA is a multiple-access method, not encryption; frames are still protected with keys from the 4-way handshake.",null],1],["cw32",0,2,"Battery-powered IoT sensors on an 802.11ax network need longer battery life. Which feature lets the AP schedule when they wake?",["BSS coloring","Target Wake Time","MU-MIMO","Transmit beamforming"],1,"TWT lets the AP and client agree wake schedules so the radio can sleep longer. BSS coloring helps with spatial reuse, not power saving.","Domain 2 – 802.11ax",["BSS coloring tags frames from each BSS to improve spatial reuse in overlapping cells; it does not schedule client wake times.",null,"MU-MIMO sends separate spatial streams to several clients at once to improve capacity, not battery life.","Transmit beamforming focuses energy toward a client to improve signal; it does not let a client sleep longer."],2],["cw33",0,3,"A capture shows beacon frames every 102.4 ms. What 802.11 frame type is a beacon?",["Null function","Control","Data","Management"],3,"Beacons, probes, authentication and association frames are management frames. Null function is a data subtype.","Domain 3 – Frame types",["Null function is a data frame subtype with no payload, often used to signal power-save state; beacons are not null frames.","Control frames such as ACK, RTS and CTS help deliver other frames; beacons announce the BSS and are management frames.","Data frames carry user payload (or null data); a beacon carries BSS information, not user data.",null],1],["cw34",0,3,"Which 802.11 frame type carries ACK, RTS and CTS frames?",["Management","Control","Data","Action"],1,"ACK, RTS, CTS and Block Ack are control frames that help deliver other frames. Action frames are a management subtype.","Domain 3 – Frame types",["Management frames include beacons, probes, authentication and association, not ACK, RTS or CTS.",null,"Data frames carry payload; ACK, RTS and CTS carry no user data and assist delivery.","Action frames are a management frame subtype used for things like Block Ack setup and 802.11k/v requests, not a separate frame type."],1],["cw35",0,3,"What is the correct order of frames when a client joins a WPA2-Personal BSS?",["Authentication, probe, 4-way handshake, association","Probe, association, 4-way handshake, authentication","Probe, authentication, association, 4-way handshake","4-way handshake, probe, authentication, association"],2,"The client discovers the BSS, performs open system authentication, associates, and then runs the 4-way handshake to derive keys.","Domain 3 – Joining a BSS",["The client cannot authenticate before discovering the BSS, and association must come before the 4-way handshake.","Open system authentication happens before association, not after the 4-way handshake.",null,"The 4-way handshake needs an association in place first, so it cannot come before probing."],2],["cw36",0,3,"A capture shows a laptop sending probe requests on each channel in turn. What is the laptop doing?",["Active scanning","Passive scanning","Reassociating","Deauthenticating"],0,"Active scanning means sending probe requests and waiting for probe responses. Passive scanning only listens for beacons.","Domain 3 – Joining a BSS",[null,"Passive scanning means listening for beacons without transmitting; sending probe requests is active scanning.","Reassociation is a request to a specific AP when roaming within an ESS, not probes sent on each channel in turn.","Deauthentication ends an existing connection and is not sent on each channel to discover networks."],1],["cw37",0,3,"A station hears a frame whose Duration field is 300 µs and holds off transmitting for that time. Which mechanism is this?",["Energy detect CCA","Virtual carrier sense (NAV)","The random backoff timer","The SIFS interval"],1,"The NAV timer is set from the Duration field of frames heard, so the station defers even if it later cannot sense energy. CCA is physical carrier sense.","Domain 3 – Medium access",["Energy detect CCA is physical carrier sense that reacts to energy currently on the channel, not a timer set from a Duration field.",null,"The random backoff timer is drawn from the contention window after the medium goes idle; it is not set by another station's Duration field.","SIFS is a fixed, very short gap before responses such as ACKs, not a 300 µs deferral taken from a heard frame."],2],["cw38",0,3,"What does an RTS/CTS exchange achieve before a data frame is sent?",["It checks the client's power save state","It encrypts the next data frame","It asks the AP for a higher data rate","It reserves the medium through the NAV"],3,"The CTS from the AP sets the NAV of every station that hears it, including hidden stations that did not hear the RTS, so they defer.","Domain 3 – Protection",["Power save state is signaled with the power management bit, PS-Poll or null frames, not RTS/CTS.","RTS/CTS carry no keys and do not encrypt anything; encryption uses keys from the 4-way handshake.","Rate selection is handled by the transmitter's rate adaptation algorithm; RTS/CTS does not negotiate data rates.",null],2],["cw39",0,3,"Which interframe space is the shortest and is used before an ACK?",["SIFS","DIFS","AIFS","EIFS"],0,"SIFS is the shortest gap, giving ACKs and CTS frames priority. DIFS and AIFS are longer and used for contention; EIFS follows a corrupted frame.","Domain 3 – Medium access",[null,"DIFS is longer than SIFS and is used by stations contending for the medium under DCF, not before an ACK.","AIFS varies by WMM access category and is used for contention, so it is longer than SIFS.","EIFS is an extended wait used after receiving a corrupted frame, making it the longest of these spaces."],1],["cw40",0,3,"With WMM enabled, voice frames reach the air sooner than best-effort frames. Why?",["Higher transmit power for voice","A dedicated voice-only channel","Shorter AIFS and smaller contention windows","A guaranteed slot in each beacon"],2,"EDCA gives the voice access category a shorter AIFS and smaller contention window, so it usually wins contention. WMM does not reserve channels or slots.","Domain 3 – QoS",["WMM does not change transmit power by access category; priority comes from contention timing.","WMM does not reserve a separate channel for voice; all access categories share the same channel.",null,"EDCA is contention-based; beacons do not reserve guaranteed transmit slots for voice frames."],2],["cw41",0,3,"A dozing phone wakes for a beacon and learns the AP is holding unicast frames for it. Which beacon field told it?",["The DS parameter set","The SSID element","The Traffic Indication Map","The supported rates element"],2,"The TIM lists association IDs with buffered unicast frames. The DS parameter set gives the channel and the SSID element names the network.","Domain 3 – Power management",["The DS parameter set element tells stations the AP's current channel, not whether frames are buffered.","The SSID element carries the network name and has nothing to do with buffered frames.",null,"The supported rates element lists the data rates the BSS supports, not buffered traffic."],2],["cw42",0,3,"Push-to-talk badges on a WLAN have long delays for multicast voice after the admin raised the DTIM interval to save battery. What explains this?",["Group frames wait for the next DTIM beacon","The TIM stops listing unicast frames","The AP drops multicast from sleeping clients","A higher DTIM forces a lower data rate"],0,"The AP buffers broadcast and multicast until a DTIM beacon, so a larger DTIM interval saves power but adds delay to group traffic.","Domain 3 – Power management",[null,"The TIM continues to list clients with buffered unicast frames in every beacon regardless of the DTIM interval.","The AP buffers group traffic for dozing clients and releases it after a DTIM beacon; it does not simply drop it.","The DTIM interval controls when buffered group frames are released, not the data rate they are sent at."],3],["cw43",0,3,"In an 802.11 WLAN, which device makes the final decision to roam?",["The RADIUS server","The access point","The WLAN controller","The client station"],3,"Roaming is client-driven. 802.11v lets the network suggest a better AP, but the client chooses whether and when to move.","Domain 3 – Roaming",["The RADIUS server authenticates users and has no role in choosing which AP a client joins.","An AP can suggest a move with 802.11v or disconnect a client, but the client decides whether and where to roam.","A controller can influence roaming with steering and 802.11v hints, but the station makes the final decision.",null],1],["cw44",0,3,"Which identifier names a single basic service set and is normally the MAC address of an AP radio?",["SSID","BSSID","ESSID","OUI"],1,"The BSSID is a 48-bit MAC-style address for one BSS. The SSID is the human-readable network name that can span many BSSs.","Domain 3 – Addressing",["The SSID is the human-readable network name, which can be shared by many BSSs, not a MAC-style identifier for one BSS.",null,"The ESSID is the network name used across an extended service set of many APs, not a single BSS.","An OUI is the first 24 bits of a MAC address that identifies the manufacturer, not a whole BSS."],1],["cw45",0,3,"In a controller-based WLAN, which protocol do lightweight APs commonly use to talk to the controller?",["BGP","LLDP","STP","CAPWAP"],3,"CAPWAP is the standard tunneling protocol for AP-to-controller control and optional data traffic. LLDP only advertises neighbor information.","Domain 3 – Devices",["BGP is an internet routing protocol between autonomous systems; it is not used between APs and a WLAN controller.","LLDP advertises device identity and capabilities to directly connected neighbors; it does not tunnel AP control traffic.","STP prevents Layer 2 loops on switched networks and has no role in AP-to-controller communication.",null],1],["cw46",0,3,"A new AP needs about 25 W at the device, and the switch supports only 802.3af. What is the minimum PoE standard the switch should support?",["802.3af (PoE)","802.3at (PoE+)","802.3ab","802.3u"],1,"802.3af provides at most 12.95 W at the device; 802.3at provides up to 25.5 W. 802.3ab and 802.3u are Ethernet speed standards, not PoE.","Domain 3 – PoE",["802.3af guarantees only 12.95 W at the device, which is well short of the 25 W this AP needs.",null,"802.3ab is the 1000BASE-T Gigabit Ethernet standard over copper and does not deliver power.","802.3u is Fast Ethernet (100 Mbps) and defines no power delivery."],2],["cw47",0,3,"A small shop has two APs, no controller and no cloud account. Each AP holds its own configuration. What kind of APs are these?",["Cloud-managed APs","Lightweight APs","Autonomous APs","Mesh portal APs"],2,"Autonomous APs contain the management, control and data planes and are configured individually. Lightweight APs need a controller.","Domain 3 – Devices",["Cloud-managed APs are configured from a cloud service, and this shop has no cloud account.","Lightweight APs depend on a controller for configuration and control, and there is no controller here.",null,"A mesh portal AP bridges wireless mesh traffic to the wired network; nothing here describes a mesh."],1],["cw48",0,3,"A parking garage has no cabling in its upper levels. The APs there must forward traffic wirelessly to an AP that has a wired uplink. Which design is this?",["Mesh","Band steering","Channel bonding","Load balancing"],0,"In a mesh, APs use wireless backhaul to reach a root or portal AP connected to the wired network. The others are radio features, not backhaul designs.","Domain 3 – Devices",[null,"Band steering nudges dual-band clients toward 5 GHz; it does not provide wireless backhaul between APs.","Channel bonding combines adjacent channels for more throughput; it does not link APs wirelessly to a wired uplink.","Load balancing spreads clients across APs; it does not carry traffic from unwired APs to a wired one."],2],["cw49",0,3,"In a capture, which frame control flag shows that a frame is a retransmission?",["More Data","Retry","Power Management","Protected Frame"],1,"The Retry bit is set when a frame is sent again. More Data tells a dozing client more frames are buffered.","Domain 3 – Frames",["More Data tells a dozing client that the AP has more buffered frames for it; it does not mark retransmissions.",null,"The Power Management bit tells the AP whether the station is going to doze, not whether the frame is a retry.","The Protected Frame bit shows that the frame body is encrypted, not that it was resent."],1],["cw50",0,3,"802.11n and later radios send several MPDUs in one A-MPDU. How does the receiver confirm them efficiently?",["A bit in the next beacon","A separate ACK for each MPDU","A CTS-to-self frame","A single Block Ack frame"],3,"Block Ack confirms many frames at once with a bitmap, keeping aggregation efficient. CTS-to-self is a protection mechanism.","Domain 3 – Aggregation",["Beacons do not acknowledge data frames; they announce the BSS and buffered traffic.","Sending a separate ACK for every MPDU would waste the airtime that aggregation is meant to save.","CTS-to-self is a protection mechanism that reserves the medium before transmitting; it does not acknowledge received frames.",null],1],["cw51",0,4,"An auditorium must serve 800 users. Which approach best fits a capacity-based design?",["More APs at lower power on 20 MHz channels","Fewer APs at maximum power for full coverage","80 MHz channels on every AP in 2.4 GHz","One high-gain omni in the center of the room"],0,"Capacity design makes smaller cells so each AP serves fewer clients and channels can be reused. Maximum power and wide channels reduce reuse.","Domain 4 – Capacity design",[null,"Fewer APs at maximum power creates large cells with many clients per AP, which suits coverage, not 800 users.","80 MHz channels do not fit in the 83.5 MHz 2.4 GHz band, and wide channels reduce reuse in a dense room anyway.","A single omni puts every user on one AP and one channel, which cannot provide the airtime 800 users need."],3],["cw52",0,4,"A hospital is designing for Wi-Fi phones. Which target is common vendor guidance?",["About −30 dBm with no SNR target","About −85 dBm with an SNR near 5 dB","About −67 dBm with an SNR near 25 dB","About −90 dBm with an SNR near 10 dB"],2,"Many voice client vendors recommend around −67 dBm and 25 dB SNR so higher rates and low retries are possible. −85 dBm is near the edge of usable signal.","Domain 4 – Design targets",["-30 dBm is an extremely strong signal found only right next to an AP, and ignoring SNR ignores noise-related failures.","-85 dBm with 5 dB SNR is near the edge of receive sensitivity and would give low rates, retries and dropped calls.",null,"-90 dBm is close to the noise floor and a 10 dB SNR supports only the lowest rates, far too poor for voice."],2],["cw53",0,4,"Why do voice designs call for each area to hear a second AP at a usable signal level?",["So the controller can load balance by SSID","So each phone can join two APs at once","So phones can roam without dropping calls","So radar detection is shared across APs"],2,"Secondary coverage gives the phone a roaming target before the current signal becomes too weak, avoiding gaps and dropped calls.","Domain 4 – Design targets",["Load balancing is not tied to SSIDs, and secondary coverage exists to give phones a roaming target, not to spread SSIDs.","An 802.11 station associates with only one AP at a time; secondary coverage does not let it join two.",null,"Radar detection is a DFS function each radio performs itself; it is not the reason for overlapping voice coverage."],2],["cw54",0,4,"Which architecture keeps configuration and monitoring in a vendor-hosted service while user traffic is forwarded locally by the APs?",["Cloud-managed","Autonomous","Centralized controller","Standalone mesh"],0,"Cloud-managed WLANs host the management plane in the cloud; data stays local. Autonomous APs have no central management at all.","Domain 4 – Architectures",[null,"Autonomous APs have no central management service; each is configured on its own.","A centralized controller hosts management on premises and often tunnels user traffic to itself, rather than a vendor-hosted service.","A standalone mesh describes wireless backhaul between APs, not where management is hosted."],1],["cw55",0,4,"Branch APs tunnel all user traffic across the WAN to a controller at headquarters, and branch users complain about slow access to a local printer. What is the best change?",["Move the branch APs to 40 MHz channels","Raise AP power at the branch office","Add another SSID for printer access","Forward branch traffic locally at the AP"],3,"Local (distributed) forwarding keeps branch traffic on the branch LAN instead of hairpinning across the WAN. Radio changes do not fix the traffic path.","Domain 4 – Data forwarding",["Wider channels change radio capacity but not the traffic path; frames would still hairpin through headquarters.","Higher AP power affects coverage, not the WAN round trip caused by tunneling traffic to a distant controller.","A new SSID would still be tunneled to headquarters unless its forwarding mode changes, and it adds beacon overhead.",null],3],["cw56",0,4,"Radio resource management that adjusts channels and power across APs belongs to which plane?",["Management plane","Control plane","Data plane","User plane"],1,"The control plane coordinates the network, such as RRM and roaming. The management plane handles configuration and monitoring; the data plane forwards user traffic.","Domain 4 – Planes",["The management plane handles configuration, firmware and monitoring, not live coordination such as RRM.",null,"The data plane forwards user frames; it does not decide channel and power settings.","The user plane is another name for the data plane that carries user traffic, not the coordination functions."],2],["cw57",0,4,"An autonomous AP will serve three SSIDs, each mapped to a different VLAN. How should the switch port for the AP be configured?",["As a routed port with no VLANs","As an access port in the guest VLAN","As an access port in the native VLAN","As an 802.1Q trunk with those VLANs"],3,"Carrying several VLANs to the AP requires an 802.1Q trunk. An access port carries only one VLAN.","Domain 4 – SSIDs and VLANs",["A routed port has no VLANs, so it cannot deliver three separate VLANs to the AP.","An access port in the guest VLAN would carry only guest traffic, leaving the other two SSIDs without their VLANs.","An access port carries only one untagged VLAN, so the SSIDs could not be mapped to different VLANs.",null],2],["cw58",0,4,"A site has eight SSIDs on every AP and high channel utilization even when few users are active. What is the likely cause?",["DFS events in the 2.4 GHz band","Beacon and probe overhead from many SSIDs","Too few spatial streams on the APs","Overly strong WPA3 encryption"],1,"Each SSID beacons separately, usually at a low basic rate, so many SSIDs consume airtime before any data flows. DFS applies to 5 GHz only.","Domain 4 – SSID design",["DFS applies to certain 5 GHz channels only; there is no DFS in 2.4 GHz.",null,"Fewer spatial streams limit peak throughput but do not create high utilization when few users are active.","Encryption strength does not add airtime; WPA3 frames are about the same size as WPA2 frames."],3],["cw59",0,4,"An admin disables the 1, 2, 5.5 and 11 Mbps rates on 2.4 GHz. What is the main benefit?",["Each AP cell becomes larger","Legacy 802.11b clients connect more easily","Frames use less airtime, which helps roaming","Beacons are no longer transmitted"],2,"Higher minimum rates shorten management frames and make cells effectively smaller, encouraging clients to roam. It locks out 802.11b clients rather than helping them.","Domain 4 – Data rates",["Raising the minimum data rate makes cells effectively smaller, not larger.","Disabling 1-11 Mbps prevents 802.11b-only clients from connecting at all.",null,"Beacons are still sent, just at a higher basic rate, so they use less airtime."],3],["cw60",0,4,"Which feature encourages dual-band clients to connect on 5 GHz instead of 2.4 GHz?",["Band steering","Load balancing","BSS coloring","Dynamic Frequency Selection"],0,"Band steering delays or ignores 2.4 GHz probe responses for dual-band clients so they join on 5 GHz. Load balancing spreads clients across APs.","Domain 4 – Client steering",[null,"Load balancing spreads clients across APs by load; it does not specifically push clients to the 5 GHz band.","BSS coloring improves spatial reuse between overlapping 802.11ax BSSs; it does not steer clients between bands.","DFS is a radar avoidance requirement on some 5 GHz channels; it does not move clients from 2.4 GHz."],1],["cw61",0,4,"A warehouse uses 1x1 scanners that support only 2.4 GHz. Why should this be found before the design is done?",["Scanners can use only DFS channels in 5 GHz","The design must suit the weakest critical client","2.4 GHz clients need 160 MHz channels","It only affects the choice of SSID name"],1,"Coverage, band and rate choices must work for the least capable devices the business depends on, so a 5 GHz-only design would fail these scanners.","Domain 4 – Requirements",["Scanners limited to 2.4 GHz cannot use 5 GHz channels at all, DFS or not.",null,"160 MHz channels do not exist in 2.4 GHz, and 1x1 scanners certainly do not need them.","Client band support drives AP placement, band and rate decisions, not just the SSID name."],3],["cw62",0,4,"A switch has a 370 W PoE budget. The plan adds 16 APs that can each draw 25.5 W. What is the concern?",["PoE budget applies only to phones","The APs need 802.3af instead","The budget suits 32 of these APs","The 408 W demand exceeds the budget"],3,"16 × 25.5 W = 408 W, above the 370 W budget, so some APs may not power up or may run in a reduced mode.","Domain 4 – PoE planning",["PoE budgets cover every powered device on the switch, including APs, not just phones.","802.3af supplies only 12.95 W at the device, far below the 25.5 W these APs can draw.","370 W divided by 25.5 W supports only about 14 of these APs at full draw, not 32.",null],3],["cw63",0,4,"Several nearby 5 GHz APs are all on the same 80 MHz channel and users see slow throughput. What design change helps most?",["Use narrower channels to allow more reuse","Increase transmit power on every AP","Add more SSIDs to split the users","Move every AP to channel 36 at 160 MHz"],0,"Narrower channels give more non-overlapping channels, reducing co-channel contention. More power or wider channels increase contention.","Domain 4 – Channel reuse",[null,"Raising power makes APs hear each other even more, increasing co-channel contention instead of reducing it.","Extra SSIDs share the same channel and add beacon overhead, so they reduce airtime rather than splitting load.","Putting every AP on one 160 MHz channel leaves no reuse at all and makes contention worse."],3],["cw64",0,5,"A small business worries that someone could capture its Wi-Fi handshake and guess the password offline. Which change helps most?",["Enable MAC address filtering","Hide the SSID from beacon frames","Move to WPA3-Personal using SAE","Switch from CCMP to TKIP"],2,"SAE resists offline dictionary attacks against a captured exchange. Hiding the SSID and MAC filtering are easily bypassed, and TKIP is deprecated.","Domain 5 – WPA3",["MAC filtering does not protect the handshake, and MAC addresses are sent in the clear and easy to spoof.","A hidden SSID still appears in probe and association frames and does nothing to stop offline passphrase guessing.",null,"TKIP is deprecated and weaker than CCMP, and it still uses the same PSK handshake that can be attacked offline."],3],["cw65",0,5,"A café wants an open network with no password but encrypted traffic for each guest. Which option fits?",["WEP with a shared key","WPA2-Personal with a posted key","Enhanced Open (OWE)","A hidden SSID"],2,"OWE gives each client unique encryption without a passphrase. A shared posted key lets anyone with it attack others' sessions.","Domain 5 – Enhanced Open",["WEP is broken and requires a shared key, so it is neither password-free nor meaningfully secure.","A posted WPA2 key lets anyone who knows it derive other guests' session keys from captured handshakes, and it is not password-free.",null,"Hiding the SSID provides no encryption at all and the name still leaks in probe frames."],2],["cw66",0,5,"In a WPA2-Enterprise network, which 802.1X role does the access point or controller play?",["Authenticator","Supplicant","Authentication server","Certificate authority"],0,"The client is the supplicant, the AP or controller is the authenticator, and RADIUS is the authentication server.","Domain 5 – 802.1X",[null,"The supplicant is the client device requesting access, not the AP or controller.","The authentication server is usually RADIUS, which checks credentials behind the AP.","A certificate authority issues certificates; it is not one of the three 802.1X roles on the WLAN."],1],["cw67",0,5,"A company wants mutual certificate authentication with no user passwords sent over Wi-Fi. Which EAP method fits?",["LEAP","PEAP-MSCHAPv2","EAP-TTLS with PAP","EAP-TLS"],3,"EAP-TLS requires certificates on both server and client. PEAP and TTLS normally use passwords inside a tunnel; LEAP is obsolete.","Domain 5 – EAP",["LEAP is an obsolete Cisco method that uses passwords (MS-CHAP) and is vulnerable to dictionary attacks.","PEAP-MSCHAPv2 uses only a server certificate and authenticates the user with a password inside the tunnel.","EAP-TTLS with PAP sends a user password inside a TLS tunnel and does not require client certificates.",null],2],["cw68",0,5,"Which encryption protocol is mandatory for WPA2?",["TKIP, based on RC4","CCMP, based on AES","WEP with 104-bit keys","Triple DES in CBC mode"],1,"WPA2 certification requires CCMP (AES). TKIP was an interim fix for WEP hardware and is now deprecated.","Domain 5 – Encryption",["TKIP was the WPA stopgap built on RC4 for older hardware; it is optional in WPA2 and now deprecated.",null,"WEP predates WPA2, is broken, and is not allowed in WPA2 at all.","Triple DES is not used by any 802.11 security suite; WPA2 is based on AES."],1],["cw69",0,5,"Clients keep dropping off an SSID, and a capture shows floods of deauthentication frames spoofing the AP's MAC address. Which feature prevents this attack?",["Band steering","Fast BSS transition (802.11r)","Neighbor reports (802.11k)","Protected Management Frames (802.11w)"],3,"802.11w protects deauthentication and disassociation frames so forged ones are ignored. 802.11r only speeds up roaming.","Domain 5 – PMF",["Band steering moves dual-band clients to 5 GHz and gives no protection against forged management frames.","802.11r speeds up roaming with pre-derived keys but does not authenticate deauthentication frames.","802.11k neighbor reports help clients choose roaming targets and do nothing against spoofed deauthentication.",null],3],["cw70",0,5,"WIPS finds an unmanaged AP plugged into a conference room wall jack on the corporate LAN. How is it classified?",["Neighbor AP","Rogue AP","Mesh portal","Managed AP"],1,"A rogue AP is unauthorized and connected to the organization's wired network. A neighbor AP belongs to someone else and is not on your LAN.","Domain 5 – Rogues and WIPS",["A neighbor AP belongs to someone else and is not connected to your wired LAN, unlike this one.",null,"A mesh portal is an authorized AP that bridges mesh traffic to the wired network, not an unmanaged device.","A managed AP is authorized and controlled by your WLAN system, which this unmanaged AP is not."],2],["cw71",0,5,"In WPA2-Personal, where does the Pairwise Master Key come from?",["The group temporal key","The RADIUS server's reply","The passphrase and SSID","The AP's BSSID alone"],2,"With a PSK, the PMK is derived from the passphrase and SSID. In Enterprise mode it comes from the EAP exchange with RADIUS.","Domain 5 – Key hierarchy",["The GTK is delivered during the 4-way handshake and is derived separately; it is not the source of the PMK.","RADIUS provides the PMK source in Enterprise mode only; WPA2-Personal uses no RADIUS server.",null,"The BSSID (AP MAC address) is used in deriving the PTK, but the PMK comes from the passphrase and SSID."],2],["cw72",0,5,"A guest SSID must stop guest devices from reaching one another. Which setting should be used?",["Client isolation","MAC filtering","SSID hiding","A longer DTIM interval"],0,"Client (peer-to-peer) isolation blocks traffic between wireless clients. MAC filtering and hiding do not stop client-to-client traffic.","Domain 5 – Guest access",[null,"MAC filtering only controls which devices may associate; it does not stop associated guests from talking to each other.","Hiding the SSID only removes the name from beacons and has no effect on traffic between clients.","The DTIM interval controls delivery of buffered group frames to dozing clients, not client-to-client traffic."],2],["cw73",0,6,"An engineer walks a site recording signal and noise from every AP without associating to any of them. Which survey is this?",["Active survey","Passive survey","Predictive survey","Spectrum-only survey"],1,"A passive survey listens to all APs. An active survey associates and measures throughput, retries and roaming.","Domain 6 – Survey types",["An active survey associates to the network to measure throughput, retries and roaming, which this engineer is not doing.",null,"A predictive survey is done in software from floor plans, without walking the site with a live adapter.","A spectrum-only survey records raw RF energy, not per-AP signal and noise from decoded Wi-Fi frames."],1],["cw74",0,6,"Before a new building is finished, a designer uses floor plans and wall attenuation values in software to plan AP locations. Which survey is this?",["Validation survey","Passive survey","Active survey","Predictive survey"],3,"A predictive survey models RF with software and building data before APs are installed. The others need measurements on site.","Domain 6 – Survey types",["A validation survey measures a live network after installation; here the building is not even finished.","A passive survey requires walking the site and hearing real APs, which is impossible before installation.","An active survey requires associating to a working network on site, which does not exist yet.",null],1],["cw75",0,6,"Users in a break room lose 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi around lunchtime, but 5 GHz is fine. Which tool best identifies the source?",["Spectrum analyzer","Protocol analyzer","Cable tester","RADIUS debug log"],0,"The likely cause is non-Wi-Fi energy such as a microwave oven, which only a spectrum analyzer shows. A protocol analyzer only decodes 802.11 frames.","Domain 6 – Spectrum analysis",[null,"A protocol analyzer decodes 802.11 frames only, so it can show retries but not a non-Wi-Fi source such as a microwave oven.","A cable tester checks wired Ethernet cabling and cannot detect RF interference.","RADIUS debug logs show authentication results, which would not explain a 2.4 GHz-only lunchtime outage."],2],["cw76",0,6,"A laptop running Wireshark shows only its own traffic and no beacons from other networks. What must change to see all 802.11 frames on a channel?",["Join every SSID to be captured","Turn on promiscuous mode on the switch port","Put the adapter in monitor mode on that channel","Use a wired mirror port on the AP uplink"],2,"Monitor mode lets a wireless adapter capture all 802.11 frames on its channel, including management and control frames. Wired mirroring never shows RF frames.","Domain 6 – Protocol analysis",["Joining SSIDs does not let the adapter see other stations' frames; it still captures only its own traffic.","Promiscuous mode on a switch port is a wired concept and does not affect what a wireless adapter hears.",null,"A wired mirror port shows Ethernet frames after the AP converts them, never beacons or other 802.11 RF frames."],2],["cw77",0,6,"Two laptops at opposite ends of a long room both connect to the central AP but cannot hear each other, and retries are high. What is the likely problem?",["Co-channel contention","Adjacent channel interference","Hidden node","Sticky client"],2,"Stations that cannot hear each other transmit at the same time and collide at the AP. RTS/CTS, AP placement or smaller cells can help.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",["Co-channel contention involves devices that hear each other and share airtime; these laptops cannot hear each other.","Adjacent channel interference comes from overlapping neighboring channels; both laptops here use the same AP and channel.",null,"A sticky client stays on a distant AP instead of roaming; these laptops are both connected to the central AP correctly."],2],["cw78",0,6,"Twelve APs in an open office are all on channel 6 at full power, and airtime is saturated. What is the best fix?",["Reduce power and spread APs across channels","Raise power on all APs to beat noise","Enable 40 MHz channels in 2.4 GHz","Add a second SSID on channel 6"],0,"The APs hear each other and must share airtime (co-channel contention). Lower power and more channels, ideally 5 GHz, restore capacity.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",[null,"Raising power makes the APs hear each other farther away, increasing co-channel contention rather than fixing it.","40 MHz in 2.4 GHz leaves only one clean channel, which would make contention worse.","A second SSID on the same channel adds beacon overhead and shares the same saturated airtime."],3],["cw79",0,6,"A client stays connected to a distant AP with a weak signal even when standing under another AP. What is this called?",["An evil twin","A hidden node","A rogue client","A sticky client"],3,"Sticky clients delay roaming. 802.11k/v, raising minimum basic rates and tuning power can encourage them to move.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",["An evil twin is a malicious AP imitating a legitimate SSID; this is a normal client failing to roam.","A hidden node is a station that other stations cannot hear, causing collisions, not one that refuses to roam.","A rogue client is an unauthorized device; this client is authorized but slow to roam.",null],1],["cw80",0,6,"A phone shows full signal bars but uploads are very slow, and the AP is set to its maximum power. What is a likely cause?",["The phone uses too high a DTIM value","The AP's power far exceeds the phone's","The AP's 2.4 GHz band is turned off","The SSID is sent in every beacon"],1,"The phone hears the strong AP, but its weaker transmissions reach the AP poorly, causing uplink retries. Matching AP power to client capability helps.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",["DTIM affects how often a dozing client wakes for group traffic, not uplink throughput.",null,"Turning off 2.4 GHz would not make a connected phone's uploads slow while it shows full bars.","Sending the SSID in beacons is normal behavior and has no effect on the phone's uplink."],3],["cw81",0,6,"A capture made next to the AP looks clean, but the user far away still complains. Where should the next capture be made?",["Next to a different AP","At the core switch","On the RADIUS server","Near the affected client"],3,"Capture close to the client on its channel to see what it hears and sends. Wired captures miss RF behavior entirely.","Domain 6 – Protocol analysis",["Another AP's location shows what that AP hears, not what the affected client is experiencing.","A core switch capture shows wired frames only and misses RF problems such as retries or interference near the client.","The RADIUS server shows authentication results, not the RF conditions the client experiences.",null],3],["cw82",0,6,"Which capture statistic most directly points to RF problems such as interference or collisions?",["A high beacon count","A high retry rate","A long DTIM interval","Many probe responses"],1,"Retransmissions mean frames were not acknowledged, often from interference, collisions or weak signal. Beacon counts are normal background traffic.","Domain 6 – Metrics",["Beacons are normal background management traffic, so a high count does not indicate interference or collisions.",null,"The DTIM interval is a configuration setting for group traffic delivery, not a symptom of RF problems.","Many probe responses usually reflect clients scanning or many SSIDs, not frame loss from interference."],2],["cw83",0,6,"After APs are installed, the customer wants proof that the design targets were met. Which activity provides this?",["A wired cable certification","A predictive design refresh","A post-installation validation survey","A new requirements interview"],2,"A validation survey measures the live network against the agreed requirements. Predictive models are estimates, not proof.","Domain 6 – Validation",["Cable certification proves the wired runs meet their category spec, not that RF design targets are met.","A predictive refresh is still a software model and cannot prove what the installed network actually delivers.",null,"A requirements interview gathers goals before design; it does not measure whether they were achieved."],2],["cw84",0,6,"All WPA2-Enterprise clients began failing authentication on the same morning with no configuration changes. PEAP logs show a server certificate error. What is the likely cause?",["The RADIUS server certificate expired","The PSK was changed overnight","The AP moved to a DFS channel","The DHCP scope ran out of leases"],0,"An expired server certificate breaks PEAP and EAP-TLS for every client at once. There is no PSK in Enterprise mode, and DHCP happens after authentication.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",[null,"WPA2-Enterprise uses 802.1X/EAP and has no PSK, so changing one could not cause this.","A DFS channel change causes a brief disconnect, not PEAP server certificate errors for every client.","DHCP happens after successful authentication, so an exhausted scope would not cause certificate errors in PEAP."],3],["cw85",0,6,"On a WPA2-Personal network, a capture shows EAPOL messages 1 and 2 but the AP never sends message 3. What is most likely wrong?",["The client's RADIUS certificate expired","The client has the wrong passphrase","The AP lacks a DHCP reservation","The AP uses a hidden SSID"],1,"The AP checks the MIC in message 2; with a mismatched passphrase it fails and the AP does not continue. Personal mode uses no RADIUS.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",["WPA2-Personal uses a pre-shared key, not RADIUS, so no RADIUS certificate is involved.",null,"DHCP runs only after the 4-way handshake succeeds, so it cannot stop message 3 from being sent.","A hidden SSID affects discovery only; the client has already associated and started the handshake."],3],["cw86",0,6,"Two neighboring 2.4 GHz APs are on channels 1 and 3. What problem does this create?",["DFS radar events","Co-channel contention only","Hidden node collisions","Adjacent channel interference"],3,"Overlapping but different channels cannot share the medium properly and corrupt each other's frames. Use 1, 6 and 11 instead.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",["DFS applies to certain 5 GHz channels, not to 2.4 GHz channels 1 and 3.","Co-channel contention happens when APs share the same channel; channels 1 and 3 are different but overlapping.","A hidden node is a station that others cannot hear; this problem comes from overlapping channel assignments.",null],2],["cw87",0,6,"During an active survey, the engineer places a temporary AP on a stand at planned locations to find the cell edge. What is this method called?",["AP-on-a-stick survey","Predictive modeling","Passive walk-through","Spectrum sweep"],0,"AP-on-a-stick uses a temporary AP to measure real coverage at candidate spots before final mounting.","Domain 6 – Survey types",[null,"Predictive modeling is done in software from floor plans, without placing a real AP on site.","A passive walk-through listens to already installed APs rather than placing a temporary AP at candidate spots.","A spectrum sweep measures RF energy and interference, not the coverage cell edge of a temporary AP."],1],["cw88",0,6,"A spectrum analyzer shows a source using nearly 100% duty cycle across channels 1 to 4. What happens to Wi-Fi devices there?",["They raise power until the noise stops","They switch to 6 GHz on their own","They defer constantly and cannot send","They ignore it because it is not 802.11"],2,"A constant non-Wi-Fi signal keeps CCA busy or ruins SNR, so devices defer or fail to decode. Remove the source or move channels.","Domain 6 – Spectrum analysis",["Wi-Fi devices cannot out-shout a constant interferer, and raising power does not clear energy another device is producing.","Clients do not move bands on their own because of interference; 6 GHz also requires 6 GHz-capable hardware.",null,"CCA energy detect reacts to any strong energy, not only 802.11 frames, so non-Wi-Fi signals still block transmissions."],3],["cw89",0,6,"A laptop associates to the corporate SSID and completes the handshake, but it gets a 169.254.x.x address. Where should troubleshooting focus next?",["The 802.11 authentication frames","The antenna's polarization","DHCP and VLAN mapping for the SSID","The AP's channel and power"],2,"The Wi-Fi link works; the problem is Layer 3 addressing, often a wrong VLAN on the SSID or trunk, or a DHCP scope or relay issue.","Domain 6 – Troubleshooting",["The 802.11 authentication and 4-way handshake already succeeded, so the link layer is working.","Polarization affects signal strength, but the laptop already has a working Wi-Fi link.",null,"Channel and power are RF settings; the laptop is connected and the failure is IP addressing."],3],["cw90",0,6,"Which channel utilization finding most suggests a cell cannot take more clients?",["Utilization stays high while busy","Utilization is low all day","Utilization rises for a moment","Utilization is the same on each band"],0,"Consistently high utilization during busy periods means little airtime is left for new clients. Brief spikes are normal.","Domain 6 – Metrics",[null,"Low utilization all day means plenty of airtime is free, so the cell can take more clients.","Brief spikes are normal as traffic bursts and do not show that the cell is out of capacity.","Equal utilization on each band says nothing about whether that utilization is high or low."],2],["x1",0,1,"Two copies of the same signal reach a receiver with equal amplitude but 180 degrees out of phase. What is the result?",["The received signal roughly doubles in strength (upfade)","The data rate rises because the copies are combined","The copies cancel each other, leaving a deep null","The signal shifts down to a lower frequency"],2,"Waves that are 180 degrees out of phase with equal amplitude cancel, so multipath can produce a null. An upfade happens only when copies arrive nearly in phase, which is the opposite case.","Domain 1: Amplitude, phase and multipath",["An upfade needs the copies to arrive nearly in phase; at 180 degrees they subtract instead of add.","Receivers without MIMO combining gain nothing from cancelling copies, and cancellation lowers the usable rate.",null,"Phase differences change how waves add together; they do not change the carrier frequency."],2],["x2",0,1,"A client datasheet lists a receive sensitivity of -82 dBm for its lowest MCS and -64 dBm for its highest MCS. At one desk it hears the AP at -70 dBm. What can the client do there?",["Decode the lowest MCS but not the highest one","Decode the highest MCS but not the lowest one","Decode neither rate, so it cannot associate","Decode both, since sensitivity applies only to APs"],0,"-70 dBm is stronger than -82 dBm but weaker than -64 dBm, so only the lower rate is supported. Reading -70 as stronger than -64 is the classic sign error with negative dBm values.","Domain 1: Receive sensitivity",[null,"-70 dBm is weaker than -64 dBm, so the signal is below what the highest MCS needs.","-70 dBm is stronger than -82 dBm, so the lowest MCS can be decoded and the client can associate.","Receive sensitivity is a property of every receiver, including client radios, not only APs."],3],["x3",0,1,"A link calculation shows a bridge's received signal will sit exactly at the radio's receive sensitivity figure. Why is this design weak?",["The EIRP must then be over the legal limit","The Fresnel zone must then be fully blocked","Receive sensitivity values apply only to omnidirectional antennas","There is no fade margin for normal signal swings"],3,"Signal varies with weather, multipath and interference, so links need fade margin above the receive sensitivity. Nothing about sitting at sensitivity implies an EIRP violation; EIRP is set at the transmit side.","Domain 1: Fade margin and link budget",["EIRP is fixed by transmitter power, cable loss and antenna gain; the receive level says nothing about exceeding it.","A received level at sensitivity does not mean the Fresnel zone is blocked; it may be clear and simply too long a link.","Receive sensitivity is a radio specification and applies whatever antenna is attached.",null],2],["x4",0,1,"An antenna datasheet lists a horizontal beamwidth of 60 degrees. What does this figure measure?",["The full angle over which the antenna radiates any usable gain at all","The angle between the points where power is 3 dB below peak","The physical width of the antenna's reflector element","The angle at which the antenna's back lobe begins"],1,"Beamwidth is measured between the half-power (-3 dB) points of the main lobe. Energy still radiates outside that angle, so it is not the full angle of usable gain.","Domain 1: Antenna beamwidth",["Antennas still radiate outside the beamwidth; the figure marks only the half-power points of the main lobe.",null,"Beamwidth is an electrical radiation measurement, not a physical dimension of the antenna.","The back lobe is a separate part of the pattern; beamwidth describes the width of the main lobe."],1],["x5",0,1,"An engineer is reading an antenna's azimuth chart. Which view of the radiation pattern does it show?",["The side view of the vertical pattern","The top-down view of the horizontal pattern","The VSWR measured across all of the supported frequencies","The polarization at each elevation angle"],1,"The azimuth chart is the horizontal pattern seen from above. The side view of the vertical pattern is the elevation chart, which is the usual mix-up.","Domain 1: Azimuth and elevation charts",["The side view of the vertical pattern is the elevation chart, not the azimuth chart.",null,"VSWR is shown in a separate impedance or return loss chart, not in the radiation pattern.","Polarization is listed as a single property of the antenna, not plotted per angle on the azimuth chart."],1],["x6",0,1,"For a point-to-point link spanning many kilometers, the engineer adds extra antenna height for earth bulge. Why?",["Longer links must always move to lower frequencies to reach farther","Earth bulge adds to free space path loss","It corrects polarization mismatch between sites","The Earth's curvature can intrude into the Fresnel zone"],3,"Over long distances the curvature of the Earth rises into the path and can block part of the Fresnel zone, so antennas are raised. Free space path loss depends on distance and frequency, not on the curvature itself.","Domain 1: Earth bulge and Fresnel zone",["Frequency choice depends on regulations and needs; earth bulge is handled by raising antennas, not changing bands.","Free space path loss comes from distance and frequency; the curvature adds an obstruction, not extra spreading loss.","Polarization mismatch comes from antenna orientation and is fixed by aligning antennas, not by height.",null],2],["x7",0,1,"An AP performs explicit transmit beamforming toward a single client. What is it doing?",["Setting per-antenna phase so the signals combine at the client","Sending a different spatial stream to several different clients at once","Physically turning a directional antenna toward the client","Raising power above the legal limit for that one client"],0,"Transmit beamforming uses channel feedback to adjust the phase of each transmit chain so the signals add constructively at the client. Serving several clients at once with separate streams is MU-MIMO, not single-user beamforming.","Domain 1: Transmit beamforming",[null,"Sending separate streams to several clients at once is MU-MIMO, not single-user beamforming.","Beamforming is done electronically by adjusting phase; nothing physically moves.","Beamforming focuses energy but must stay within regulatory power limits."],2],["x8",0,1,"An 802.11ac Wave 2 AP sends data to three single-stream smartphones at the same moment on the same channel. Which feature is it using?",["Channel bonding","Single-user MIMO","Downlink MU-MIMO","Maximal ratio combining"],2,"Downlink MU-MIMO lets an AP send separate spatial streams to several clients simultaneously. Single-user MIMO sends multiple streams to one client, and these phones each support only one stream.","Domain 1: MU-MIMO",["Channel bonding widens the channel for one transmission; it does not serve several clients at once.","Single-user MIMO sends multiple streams to one client, and these phones support only one stream each.",null,"Maximal ratio combining is a receive technique that combines copies at one radio, not a multi-client method."],2],["x9",0,1,"As a client walks away from its AP, its rate falls from a 256-QAM MCS to a 16-QAM MCS. Why does the radio step down?",["16-QAM uses a wider channel that carries farther","256-QAM is allowed only in the 6 GHz band","Lower-order modulation needs less SNR to decode","The AP switches distant clients to DSSS"],2,"Dense constellations like 256-QAM need a high SNR, so as signal drops the radio picks a lower-order modulation it can still decode. Channel width is independent of modulation, so the wider-channel claim is false.","Domain 1: Modulation and MCS",["Channel width and modulation are separate choices; 16-QAM does not imply a wider channel.","256-QAM is used in 5 GHz by 802.11ac and later, not restricted to 6 GHz.",null,"An 802.11ac/ax client does not drop to DSSS; it moves to a lower OFDM MCS."],2],["x10",0,2,"Vendors recommend placing 6 GHz APs on Preferred Scanning Channels (PSCs). What is the main reason?",["Clients can limit probing to PSCs, so they find APs faster","PSCs are the only 6 GHz channels that are exempt from any DFS rules","PSCs allow higher power than other 6 GHz channels","Only PSCs support WPA3 in the 6 GHz band"],0,"The 6 GHz band has many channels, so clients may probe only on PSCs; APs placed on PSCs are discovered faster. WPA3 or Enhanced Open is required across all 6 GHz operation, not only on PSCs.","Domain 2: 6 GHz Preferred Scanning Channels",[null,"DFS is a 5 GHz radar rule; PSCs exist to speed client discovery, not to avoid DFS.","Power limits in 6 GHz depend on device class and rules, not on whether a channel is a PSC.","WPA3 or Enhanced Open is required on every 6 GHz channel, not only on PSCs."],2],["x11",0,2,"A controller wants to suggest that a client on a busy AP move to a better AP. Which amendment defines the BSS Transition Management request it sends?",["802.11r","802.11k","802.11e","802.11v"],3,"802.11v defines BSS Transition Management, letting the network suggest a roam. 802.11k gives clients neighbor reports but does not ask them to move.","Domain 2: 802.11v",["802.11r speeds up the roam itself with fast BSS transition but does not let the network suggest a move.","802.11k supplies neighbor reports on request; it does not include the transition request itself.","802.11e defines QoS (the basis of WMM), not roaming suggestions.",null],1],["x12",0,2,"A vendor markets a new AP as Wi-Fi 6E. What does that tell a buyer?",["It supports 802.11be multi-link operation","It is 802.11ax extended into the 6 GHz band","It runs 802.11ac rates on 2.4 GHz only","It is certified for 802.11ad operation in the 60 GHz band"],1,"Wi-Fi 6E is the Wi-Fi Alliance name for 802.11ax devices that also operate in 6 GHz. Multi-link operation belongs to 802.11be, which is Wi-Fi 7.","Domain 2: Wi-Fi generation names",["Multi-link operation belongs to 802.11be (Wi-Fi 7), not to Wi-Fi 6E.",null,"802.11ac is Wi-Fi 5 and operates in 5 GHz, and it has nothing to do with the 6E name.","802.11ad is a separate 60 GHz standard and is not what the Wi-Fi 6E name refers to."],1],["x13",0,2,"Which set of features is associated with 802.11be (Wi-Fi 7)?",["TKIP and the first use of BSS coloring","320 MHz channels, 4096-QAM and multi-link operation","22 MHz DSSS channels in 2.4 GHz only","The first MIMO and 40 MHz channels"],1,"802.11be adds 320 MHz channels in 6 GHz, 4096-QAM and multi-link operation. MIMO and 40 MHz channels first arrived with 802.11n (HT).","Domain 2: 802.11be features",["TKIP is a deprecated WPA feature and BSS coloring was introduced in 802.11ax, not 802.11be.",null,"22 MHz DSSS channels in 2.4 GHz describe 802.11b, the oldest PHY in this list.","MIMO and 40 MHz channels first arrived with 802.11n (HT), well before Wi-Fi 7."],1],["x14",0,2,"An 802.11g/n AP sees an 802.11b-only client associate. What changes for the OFDM clients in the BSS?",["They must send all of their data frames with DSSS rates","They lose the ability to use WPA2","They must move to 2.4 GHz channel 14","They begin using protection before OFDM frames"],3,"The AP signals protection in its ERP element, so OFDM stations use RTS/CTS or CTS-to-self that the DSSS client can understand. They keep sending data with OFDM; only the protection frames use a legacy rate.","Domain 2: ERP protection",["OFDM stations keep sending data with OFDM; only the protection frames use a rate the DSSS client understands.","Protection mode has no effect on which security methods can be used in the BSS.","Channel 14 is not a protection mechanism and is not allowed for OFDM in most regions.",null],2],["x15",0,2,"What purpose does Transmit Power Control (TPC) serve in regulated 5 GHz bands?",["Letting devices lower power to reduce interference with other users","Raising client power automatically to match the AP","Detecting radar and moving the AP to a new channel","Letting the AP exceed its EIRP limit when idle"],0,"TPC lets devices reduce transmit power so they interfere less with other users of the band, such as radar or satellite systems. Detecting radar and changing channel is DFS, a separate requirement.","Domain 2: DFS and TPC",[null,"TPC lets devices reduce power where appropriate; it does not force clients to match AP power.","Detecting radar and changing channel is DFS, a related but separate requirement.","No mechanism lets an AP exceed its EIRP limit; TPC is about staying lower, not higher."],1],["x16",0,2,"In 5 GHz, a 40 MHz channel is formed by bonding channels 36 and 40. Which channel number is commonly used to label the bonded channel's center?",["36","40","38","44"],2,"Channel numbers are 5 MHz apart, so the center of 36 and 40 bonded together is channel 38. Channel 36 is only the primary or lower 20 MHz channel, not the center.","Domain 2: 5 GHz channel numbering",["Channel 36 is the lower 20 MHz channel in the pair, not the center of the 40 MHz channel.","Channel 40 is the upper 20 MHz channel in the pair, not the center.",null,"Channel 44 lies outside the 36 and 40 pair and belongs to the next 40 MHz channel."],1],["x17",0,2,"In 802.11ax, how does an AP coordinate an uplink OFDMA transmission from several clients?",["Each client picks a random resource unit after waiting a DIFS","Clients send RTS frames listing their resource units","The AP sends a trigger frame assigning resource units","The AP gives each client a fixed slot in the beacon"],2,"Uplink OFDMA is scheduled by the AP with a trigger frame that tells each client which resource unit to use and when. Clients do not choose units themselves after normal contention.","Domain 2: 802.11ax OFDMA",["Uplink OFDMA is scheduled by the AP; clients do not pick resource units on their own after normal contention.","RTS frames do not carry resource unit assignments; the AP decides the allocation.",null,"Beacons do not assign OFDMA resource units; trigger frames do this per transmission."],2],["x18",0,2,"An AP model is sold in several countries. Why must installers set the correct country code?",["It sets the allowed channels and power for local rules","It picks which Wi-Fi Alliance certification program applies to it","It changes which IEEE amendment the AP runs","It sets the language used in the SSID name"],0,"The country code loads the regulatory domain: legal channels, power limits and DFS rules. Wi-Fi Alliance certification is a property of the product, not something the country setting selects.","Domain 2: Regulatory domains",[null,"Wi-Fi Alliance certification is earned by the product during testing, not chosen by a country setting.","The IEEE amendments supported are fixed by the hardware; the country code only applies local rules.","SSID names are free text chosen by the admin and unrelated to the regulatory country code."],1],["x19",0,2,"Two 802.11ax APs on the same channel use different BSS colors. How can this help a station in one of the BSSs?",["It encrypts frames sent by the other BSS","It moves the other BSS to a different, non-overlapping channel automatically","It lets stations stop listening to beacons","It can apply a higher CCA threshold to the other BSS's frames"],3,"BSS coloring supports spatial reuse: a station can treat weak frames with another color using a less sensitive threshold and transmit anyway. It does not change channels; the APs still share the channel.","Domain 2: BSS coloring",["BSS color is a small identifier, not an encryption mechanism; security still comes from WPA2 or WPA3.","Coloring does not change channels; both BSSs stay on the shared channel and use spatial reuse instead.","Stations still rely on beacons for timing and parameters; coloring does not remove that need.",null],3],["x20",0,2,"Why do 2.4 GHz channels 1 and 2 interfere with each other?",["Channel 2 is reserved for radar use","Centers are 5 MHz apart but each signal is about 20 MHz wide","Both channels are limited to DSSS only","They use opposite antenna polarization"],1,"2.4 GHz channel centers are only 5 MHz apart, while each transmission is roughly 20 to 22 MHz wide, so neighboring channels overlap. Radar and DFS rules apply in parts of 5 GHz, not to channel 2.","Domain 2: 2.4 GHz channel overlap",["Radar and DFS rules apply to parts of 5 GHz, not to 2.4 GHz channel 2.",null,"OFDM (802.11g/n) runs on these channels too, and sharing a PHY type is not what causes overlap.","Channels have no fixed polarization; that is a property of the antennas."],1],["x21",0,2,"An AP must support old 802.11a clients. Which band and PHY family does 802.11a use?",["2.4 GHz with DSSS","5 GHz with OFDM","2.4 GHz with ERP-OFDM","6 GHz with OFDMA"],1,"802.11a defined OFDM in the 5 GHz band. ERP-OFDM is the 802.11g version of OFDM in 2.4 GHz, which is the tempting mix-up.","Domain 2: 802.11 PHYs",["DSSS in 2.4 GHz describes 802.11 and 802.11b, not 802.11a.",null,"ERP-OFDM is 802.11g's use of OFDM in 2.4 GHz, a different amendment and band.","OFDMA and 6 GHz arrived with 802.11ax, long after 802.11a."],1],["x22",0,3,"A client sends a data frame through its AP to a server on the wired LAN (To DS = 1, From DS = 0). Which address is in the Address 1 (receiver) field?",["The server's MAC address","The client's MAC address","The default gateway's IP address","The AP's BSSID"],3,"Address 1 is the receiver address, and the radio that receives the frame over the air is the AP, so it holds the BSSID. The server's MAC is the final destination and goes in Address 3.","Domain 3: Frame addressing",["The server's MAC is the final destination and belongs in Address 3 for a To DS frame.","The client is the transmitter, so its MAC is in Address 2, not the receiver address.","802.11 headers carry MAC addresses only; IP addresses are inside the payload.",null],3],["x23",0,3,"In a WPA2-Enterprise BSS, a capture shows two Open System authentication frames before association. What do they accomplish?",["A formality; real authentication comes after association","They check the user's password with the RADIUS server","They deliver the PMK from the AP to the client","They derive the GTK used for multicast traffic"],0,"Open System authentication performs no identity check; 802.1X/EAP and the 4-way handshake follow association. RADIUS is not contacted during these two frames.","Domain 3: Joining a BSS",[null,"RADIUS is contacted during 802.1X after association, not during the Open System frames.","The PMK comes from the EAP exchange and is never sent over the air in these frames.","The GTK is delivered in the 4-way handshake, which happens after association and EAP."],2],["x24",0,3,"A capture shows a client sending null data frames with the Power Management bit set to 1. What is the client doing?",["Asking the AP for a higher data rate","Roaming to another BSS","Telling the AP it is going into power save","Sending a keepalive message to the RADIUS server"],2,"Null data frames carry no payload and are used to signal power state; the bit set to 1 tells the AP to buffer frames. Roaming would show reassociation frames, not null data.","Domain 3: Power management",["Data rates are chosen by rate adaptation; null data frames are not rate requests.","Roaming would show reassociation frames to a new AP, not null data frames to the current one.",null,"Clients never talk to the RADIUS server directly with 802.11 frames; the AP relays EAP."],2],["x25",0,3,"A station's frame goes unacknowledged. How does DCF change its next random backoff?",["It sets the backoff to zero and retries at once","It switches to PCF and waits to be polled by the access point","It roughly doubles its contention window, up to a maximum","It waits for the next DTIM beacon to retry"],2,"After a failed transmission the contention window grows (exponential backoff) up to CWmax, lowering the chance of another collision. Retrying with zero backoff would make repeat collisions more likely.","Domain 3: Random backoff and contention window",["Retrying with zero backoff would make another collision more likely, so DCF does not do it.","PCF is an optional, rarely used mode; a failed frame does not switch a station into it.",null,"DTIM timing governs delivery of buffered multicast, not unicast retransmission."],2],["x26",0,3,"Before transmitting, a station checks whether the medium is busy by detecting a decodable 802.11 preamble or raw RF energy. What is this mechanism called?",["Clear channel assessment","Network allocation vector","Dynamic frequency selection","Transmit power control"],0,"Clear channel assessment is physical carrier sense using signal detect and energy detect. The NAV is virtual carrier sense, based on Duration values in frames rather than on sensing RF.","Domain 3: CSMA/CA and CCA",[null,"The NAV is virtual carrier sense that uses Duration values in frames, not RF energy sensing.","DFS detects radar and changes channel; it is not the per-frame medium check.","TPC adjusts transmit power and does not decide whether the medium is busy."],1],["x27",0,3,"An ERP AP must protect OFDM frames from nearby 802.11b stations. When is RTS/CTS a better choice than CTS-to-self?",["When every station hears every other station well","When the goal is the lowest possible overhead","When no 802.11b stations are present at all","When some stations cannot hear the sender"],3,"With RTS/CTS the receiver's CTS reaches stations that cannot hear the sender, which handles hidden nodes. CTS-to-self has less overhead, so it wins when overhead matters and everyone can hear the sender.","Domain 3: Protection mechanisms",["When everyone hears the sender, CTS-to-self already reaches all stations with less overhead.","RTS/CTS adds an extra frame exchange, so it is not the lowest-overhead option.","Without 802.11b stations, ERP protection is usually not needed at all.",null],3],["x28",0,3,"Wi-Fi phones must save battery but still get voice frames with low delay. Which method lets the phone send a trigger frame to pull buffered frames from the AP?",["Legacy PS-Poll","U-APSD (WMM Power Save)","DTIM multicast delivery","Spatial multiplexing power save"],1,"U-APSD lets a client's own uplink frames trigger delivery of buffered downlink frames, which suits voice. Legacy PS-Poll retrieves only one buffered frame per poll and adds delay.","Domain 3: U-APSD",["Legacy PS-Poll retrieves one buffered frame per poll, which adds delay for voice.",null,"DTIM delivery is for buffered broadcast and multicast frames, not triggered unicast voice.","SM power save turns off extra radio chains; it does not retrieve buffered frames."],2],["x29",0,3,"Which WMM access category has the lowest priority for medium access?",["Voice","Background","Video","Best effort"],1,"Background has the longest AIFS and largest contention window of the four WMM categories. Best effort is the default category but still ranks above background.","Domain 3: WMM access categories",["Voice has the highest priority, with the shortest AIFS and smallest contention window.",null,"Video is the second-highest priority category, not the lowest.","Best effort is the default category but still gets priority over background."],1],["x30",0,3,"Why does A-MPDU aggregation raise throughput on 802.11n and later radios?",["It lets several clients transmit at the same instant","It forces the radio to use 1024-QAM","It removes the need for any acknowledgment","Many frames share one contention period and preamble"],3,"Aggregation sends many MPDUs after a single contention and PHY preamble, cutting per-frame overhead. Acknowledgment is still needed; it is just done with a Block Ack.","Domain 3: Aggregation and Block Ack",["Serving several clients at once is OFDMA or MU-MIMO; aggregation packs frames for one receiver.","Aggregation works with any MCS and does not force a particular modulation.","Aggregated frames are still acknowledged, using a single Block Ack.",null],2],["x31",0,3,"A client moves between two APs that belong to the same ESS. Which frame does it send to the new AP to join it?",["Reassociation request","Probe response","Disassociation","Beacon"],0,"When roaming within an ESS the client sends a reassociation request to the new AP. Probe responses and beacons are sent by APs, not by the client.","Domain 3: Roaming and reassociation",[null,"Probe responses are sent by APs to clients during scanning, not by roaming clients.","Disassociation ends an association; it is not how a client joins the new AP.","Beacons are transmitted by APs to announce a BSS, not by clients."],1],["x32",0,3,"Why do clients typically use passive scanning on 5 GHz DFS channels?",["Passive scanning is always faster than active scanning","Beacons on DFS channels carry the network's PMK","They should not transmit there until they hear an AP","Probe requests are encrypted on DFS channels"],2,"Clients should not transmit on a DFS channel until they hear an AP operating there, so they listen for beacons. Passive scanning is usually slower, since the client must wait for beacons.","Domain 3: Passive and active scanning",["Passive scanning is usually slower, because the client must wait on each channel for a beacon.","Beacons never carry the PMK; keys are derived during authentication and the handshake.",null,"Probe requests are unencrypted management frames on every channel."],2],["x33",0,3,"A new AP's datasheet says it needs about 40 W for full operation. Which PoE standard should the switch port support?",["802.3af","802.3at","802.3bt","802.3ad"],2,"802.3at (PoE+) delivers up to 25.5 W at the device, so a 40 W AP needs 802.3bt. 802.3ad is link aggregation and has nothing to do with power.","Domain 3: PoE standards",["802.3af supplies about 12.95 W at the device, far below 40 W.","802.3at (PoE+) supplies up to 25.5 W at the device, still short of 40 W.",null,"802.3ad is link aggregation and has nothing to do with Power over Ethernet."],2],["x34",0,4,"A classroom will have 60 students each streaming video at the same time. Which kind of requirement will mainly drive the number of APs?",["Capacity requirements","Coverage requirements","The regulatory domain","Cable run lengths"],0,"Many simultaneous heavy users make airtime the limit, so AP count is set by capacity. One AP may cover a single classroom, so coverage alone would underbuild it.","Domain 4: Coverage vs capacity",[null,"One AP might cover a classroom, so designing for coverage alone would leave too little airtime for 60 streams.","The regulatory domain sets allowed channels and power, not how many APs the users need.","Cable length limits where APs can go, but does not decide how many are needed."],2],["x35",0,4,"A lecture center with many APs must get the most total capacity out of 5 GHz. Which channel width choice is usually best?",["160 MHz on every AP for top per-client rates","80 MHz on every AP so each cell is faster","40 MHz in 2.4 GHz and 80 MHz in 5 GHz","20 MHz, to get more channels for reuse"],3,"Narrow channels give more non-overlapping channels, reducing co-channel contention, which matters most in dense designs. Wide channels raise one client's peak rate but force APs to share channels.","Domain 4: Channel reuse and width",["160 MHz leaves very few channels, so nearby APs would share them and contend for airtime.","80 MHz still limits reuse in a dense space and increases co-channel contention.","40 MHz in 2.4 GHz leaves no clean channel reuse and is poor practice in dense spaces.",null],3],["x36",0,4,"One old client connected at a very low data rate slows every other client on its AP. Why?",["It forces all other clients down to its own channel width","It needs far more airtime to send the same data","It disables WMM for the whole BSS","It makes the AP lower its transmit power"],1,"Airtime is shared, and a slow client occupies the medium much longer per byte, leaving less time for others. Channel width is set per BSS and is not dictated by one client.","Domain 4: Airtime",["Channel width is set per BSS by the AP, not dictated by one slow client.",null,"WMM stays enabled for the BSS regardless of one client's data rate.","APs do not lower their transmit power because a slow client associates."],2],["x37",0,4,"In a stadium, designers mount downward-facing patch antennas instead of omnis. What is the main reason?",["Patch antennas do not need any PoE","They shape small cells so channels can be reused more","Omni antennas cannot work in 5 GHz","Patch antennas raise the legal EIRP limit"],1,"Directional antennas confine each cell to a section of seats, so the same channels can be reused nearby. EIRP limits are set by regulation and are not raised by antenna choice.","Domain 4: High-density design",["Patch antennas used with APs still need power for the AP; antenna type does not remove PoE needs.",null,"Omni antennas work fine in 5 GHz; the issue is that their wide pattern limits reuse.","EIRP limits are set by regulators and are not raised by choosing a patch antenna."],2],["x38",0,4,"In a controller-based WLAN, sharing cached keys and coordinating roams between APs belongs to which plane?",["Management plane","Data plane","User plane","Control plane"],3,"Coordination between devices, such as roaming support, key caching and RRM, is control plane work. The management plane is configuration and monitoring by administrators.","Domain 4: Management, control and data planes",["The management plane covers configuration and monitoring by administrators, not device-to-device coordination.","The data plane forwards user traffic; roam coordination is a separate control function.","User plane is another name for the data plane, which only moves user traffic.",null],2],["x39",0,4,"In a cloud-managed WLAN, the site's internet link to the cloud service fails. What usually happens to clients that are already connected?",["They keep working, since APs forward traffic locally","They drop, because the data plane lives in the cloud","They can reach only the cloud dashboard","They move to a mesh link automatically"],0,"Cloud-managed designs keep the data plane at the AP, so local traffic keeps flowing; mainly management and cloud-dependent features are lost. User data does not pass through the cloud service.","Domain 4: Cloud-managed architecture",[null,"In cloud-managed designs user traffic is forwarded locally by the APs, not through the cloud.","The dashboard is exactly what becomes unreachable when the internet link fails.","Mesh is a separate feature that must be designed in; it is not a failover for cloud reachability."],3],["x40",0,4,"In a lecture hall with four APs, the AP near the door holds most clients while the others sit idle. Which feature addresses this?",["Band steering","Dynamic Frequency Selection","Load balancing between APs","BSS coloring"],2,"Load balancing spreads clients across APs that can serve them. Band steering moves clients between bands on the same AP, which does not fix an uneven spread between APs.","Domain 4: Load balancing",["Band steering moves clients between bands on the same AP, not between APs.","DFS handles radar avoidance in 5 GHz and has no role in balancing clients.",null,"BSS coloring aids spatial reuse on shared channels and does not move clients."],2],["x41",0,4,"A designer wants one 802.1X SSID instead of separate SSIDs for staff, contractors and IoT, yet each group must land in its own VLAN. How is this done?",["Put each group of users on its own separate channel","Use MAC filtering on the AP","Have RADIUS return a VLAN for each user","Hide the SSID for each group"],2,"RADIUS can return a VLAN attribute after authentication, placing users in different VLANs on one SSID and cutting SSID overhead. MAC filtering only allows or blocks devices and does not assign VLANs.","Domain 4: SSID and VLAN design",["Channels are an RF property of APs; putting groups on different channels does not assign VLANs.","MAC filtering only allows or blocks devices and cannot choose a VLAN.",null,"Hiding the SSID only removes it from beacons; it cannot separate groups into VLANs."],3],["x42",0,4,"A tri-radio AP needs more power than the 802.3at switch port can supply. What commonly happens?",["It may run in a reduced mode, e.g. with fewer radio chains","It draws the extra power and damages the switch","It pulls power from a second switch port automatically","It disables WPA3 until more power is available"],0,"Many APs detect the power class available and disable features such as radio chains or ports. PoE negotiation prevents a device from simply drawing more than the port offers.","Domain 4: PoE planning",[null,"PoE negotiation limits power to what the port offers, so the AP cannot simply overdraw it.","APs do not automatically combine power from another port unless specifically designed and cabled for it.","PoE budget has no connection to which security protocols the AP supports."],2],["x43",0,5,"Why is WEP considered broken?",["It used AES with a key that was too short","It required a RADIUS server to work","It could be used only in 2.4 GHz","RC4 with a short, reused IV lets attackers recover the key"],3,"WEP used RC4 with a 24-bit IV that repeats, so captured traffic lets attackers recover the key. WEP never used AES; AES arrived with CCMP in WPA2.","Domain 5: Legacy weaknesses",["WEP used RC4, not AES; AES arrived with CCMP in WPA2.","WEP used static shared keys and did not require RADIUS.","WEP could be used in any band; its weakness was cryptographic, not band-related.",null],1],["x44",0,5,"A company enables WPA3-Personal transition mode so older devices can still join. What is the main security trade-off?",["WPA3 devices must fall back to TKIP","Clients can be downgraded to WPA2-PSK and its risks","PMF is turned off for every device","The passphrase must become shorter"],1,"Transition mode still accepts WPA2-PSK, so an attacker can push clients to WPA2 and capture a handshake for offline guessing. TKIP is not part of WPA3 transition mode.","Domain 5: SAE and transition mode",["WPA3 does not use TKIP; transition mode allows WPA2 with CCMP, not TKIP.",null,"PMF is required for WPA3 clients and optional for WPA2 clients in transition mode; it is not turned off for all.","Transition mode does not force a shorter passphrase; the same passphrase works for both methods."],3],["x45",0,5,"Which key encrypts broadcast and multicast frames in a WPA2 BSS?",["PTK","GTK","PMK","PSK"],1,"The Group Temporal Key protects broadcast and multicast frames and is shared by all stations in the BSS. The PTK is unique to one client and protects its unicast frames.","Domain 5: PMK, PTK and GTK",["The PTK is unique to one client and protects that client's unicast traffic.",null,"The PMK is the root key used to derive the PTK; it does not encrypt frames directly.","The PSK is the configured passphrase-based secret, not the frame encryption key for group traffic."],1],["x46",0,5,"Laptops use PEAP-MSCHAPv2 but are set not to validate the server certificate. What risk does this create?",["The 4-way handshake is skipped, so no PTK or GTK is ever derived","Clients fall back to WEP encryption","The AP can no longer assign VLANs","An evil twin with a fake RADIUS server can capture credentials"],3,"Without server certificate validation a client will run PEAP with an impostor, exposing the inner MSCHAPv2 exchange to offline cracking. The 4-way handshake still happens after EAP succeeds.","Domain 5: EAP methods and certificates",["The 4-way handshake still runs after EAP; skipping certificate checks does not remove it.","Clients do not fall back to WEP; the weakness is trusting any server during EAP.","VLAN assignment from RADIUS is unaffected by whether clients validate the certificate.",null],3],["x47",0,5,"A hotel guest SSID uses a captive portal but no Wi-Fi encryption. What does the portal provide?",["Login or terms acceptance, not encryption","AES encryption of all guest traffic","Mutual certificate authentication","Protection against evil twin APs"],0,"A captive portal controls access at the web layer; the air link stays open unless OWE or WPA is added. It does not encrypt frames, which is the most tempting misconception.","Domain 5: Guest access and captive portals",[null,"A captive portal works at the web layer and adds no Wi-Fi encryption; OWE or WPA would be needed.","Mutual certificate authentication is EAP-TLS, which a web portal does not provide.","An attacker can clone an open SSID and its portal page, so it gives no evil twin protection."],2],["x48",0,5,"An organization wants a tunneled EAP method that needs only a server certificate and can carry legacy inner methods such as PAP. Which fits?",["EAP-TLS","LEAP","EAP-TTLS","EAP-MD5"],2,"EAP-TTLS builds a TLS tunnel with a server certificate and can carry many inner methods, including PAP. EAP-TLS needs a client certificate too and carries no inner password method.","Domain 5: EAP-TTLS",["EAP-TLS requires client certificates and does not carry an inner password method like PAP.","LEAP is a legacy method with no TLS tunnel and known weaknesses.",null,"EAP-MD5 has no tunnel and no server certificate, and it offers no mutual authentication."],2],["x49",0,6,"An engineer wants to see how an interferer's energy changes over time across the band. Which spectrum analyzer view fits best?",["The real-time FFT plot","The duty cycle gauge","The waterfall (spectrogram) view","The channel utilization table"],2,"A waterfall plots frequency against time with color for amplitude, showing how energy changes. The real-time FFT shows only the current sweep, not history.","Domain 6: Spectrum analysis views",["The real-time FFT shows only the current sweep, not how energy changes over time.","A duty cycle gauge gives a percentage of time busy, not a time history across frequencies.",null,"Channel utilization tables summarize airtime use, not the shape of an interferer over time."],1],["x50",0,6,"A spectrum analyzer shows narrow bursts hopping rapidly across the whole 2.4 GHz band. Which source is most likely?",["A Bluetooth device","A microwave oven","An 802.11 AP on channel 6","A 5 GHz weather radar"],0,"Bluetooth uses narrow channels and frequency hopping across the 2.4 GHz band. A microwave oven produces a wide signal in a fixed part of the band rather than hopping.","Domain 6: Identifying non-Wi-Fi interferers",[null,"A microwave oven produces wide energy in a fixed region of the band rather than narrow hops.","An AP on channel 6 stays on one 20 MHz channel and does not hop across the band.","Weather radar operates in 5 GHz and would not appear in a 2.4 GHz view."],2],["x51",0,6,"A spectrum view shows a wide, pulsing signal in the upper part of the 2.4 GHz band that appears only at lunchtime near the break room. What is the most likely source?",["A Bluetooth headset","An 802.11ax AP","A 5 GHz cordless phone","A microwave oven"],3,"Microwave ovens leak wide, pulsed energy centered around 2.45 GHz, matching the timing and location. Bluetooth would appear as narrow hops across the whole band.","Domain 6: Identifying non-Wi-Fi interferers",["Bluetooth appears as narrow hops across the whole band, not a wide pulsed signal near one end.","An 802.11ax AP shows steady OFDM channel shapes, not bursts tied to an appliance.","A 5 GHz phone would not show up in a 2.4 GHz spectrum view at all.",null],2],["x52",0,6,"An engineer must capture a phone roaming between APs on channels 1 and 11. What capture setup works best?",["One adapter locked on channel 1 only","One monitor-mode adapter per channel, capturing together","A wired capture on one AP's uplink port","An adapter in managed mode joined to the SSID"],1,"A roam spans two channels, so a monitor-mode adapter on each captures both sides at once. A wired uplink capture misses 802.11 management frames entirely.","Domain 6: Protocol analysis",["An adapter on channel 1 alone misses everything after the phone moves to channel 11.",null,"A wired uplink capture never sees 802.11 management frames such as reassociation.","In managed mode the adapter captures only its own traffic, not the phone's frames."],3],["x53",0,6,"A voice design targets 25 dB SNR. At one spot the signal is -70 dBm and the noise floor is -90 dBm. What should the report say?",["It meets the target with 5 dB to spare","It falls 5 dB short of the target","It meets the target exactly","SNR cannot be found from these values"],1,"SNR is signal minus noise: -70 - (-90) = 20 dB, which is 5 dB below the target. Assuming a -70 dBm signal is automatically good enough for voice ignores the noise floor.","Domain 6: Key metrics",["Signal minus noise is 20 dB, which is below the 25 dB target, not above it.",null,"20 dB and 25 dB differ by 5 dB, so the target is not met exactly.","SNR is simply signal minus noise in dB, and both values are given."],2],["x54",0,6,"Only one laptop model disconnects often; other devices in the same spot are fine. What is the best first step?",["Raise transmit power on every AP in that area of the building","Move every AP to a new channel","Replace the RADIUS server certificate","Check and update that model's Wi-Fi driver"],3,"A problem confined to one model points to the client side, and outdated drivers are a common cause. Changing AP power or channels affects everyone and ignores the evidence that others are fine.","Domain 6: Client problems",["Raising power on all APs affects everyone and ignores that other devices in the same spot work fine.","Changing every AP's channel is a broad change with no evidence that the channel is the cause.","A certificate problem would affect all 802.1X clients, not one model that intermittently disconnects.",null],2],["x55",0,6,"Two APs in adjacent rooms are both on 5 GHz channel 36 at 20 MHz and hear each other clearly. What problem does this cause?",["Co-channel contention","Adjacent channel interference","Hidden node collisions","Polarization mismatch"],0,"APs on the same channel that hear each other must share airtime, which is co-channel contention. Adjacent channel interference involves overlapping but different channels.","Domain 6: Common RF problems",[null,"Adjacent channel interference needs overlapping but different channels; these APs share one channel.","Hidden nodes cannot hear each other, while these APs hear each other clearly.","Polarization mismatch is about antenna orientation and does not explain two APs sharing airtime."],1],["x56",0,6,"Clients at opposite ends of a large room cannot hear each other and collide at the AP. Which change removes the underlying cause?",["Use a longer DTIM interval","Widen the AP's channel","Add or move APs so each cell's clients hear each other","Hide the SSID in beacons"],2,"Hidden nodes exist because clients are too far apart to sense each other; smaller cells fix that. A longer DTIM interval only affects power save and multicast delivery.","Domain 6: Hidden node",["DTIM affects buffered multicast and power save, not whether clients can hear each other.","A wider channel does not make distant clients audible to each other and can reduce reuse.",null,"Hiding the SSID changes beacons only and does nothing about clients hearing one another."],3],["x57",0,6,"An engineer has fixed a recurring roaming problem. In a structured troubleshooting process, what should come last?",["Undo the fix to prove that it was what caused the improvement","Close the ticket without any notes","Document the problem, cause, fix and results","Reboot all APs to be safe"],2,"Documenting findings helps future troubleshooting and confirms the resolution. Undoing a working fix would bring back the outage for users without adding value.","Domain 6: Structured troubleshooting",["Undoing a working fix brings the outage back for users and is not a standard final step.","Closing without notes loses what was learned and makes a repeat harder to solve.",null,"Rebooting every AP disrupts users and adds nothing once the problem is solved."],1],["x58",0,6,"During a validation survey, one area shows -65 dBm signal with a -80 dBm noise floor. What should the engineer report?",["Poor SNR (15 dB) despite the good signal","Excellent conditions for voice calls","The AP's transmit power is too high","Co-channel contention from too many SSIDs"],0,"The signal level looks healthy, but a raised noise floor leaves only 15 dB of SNR, which is too low for voice and points to interference. Judging by signal alone would wrongly call it excellent.","Domain 6: Validation surveys",[null,"15 dB of SNR is too low for voice, so conditions are not excellent despite a good signal.","Strong AP power would raise the signal, not the noise floor; the noise points to interference.","SSID overhead raises channel utilization; it does not raise the measured noise floor."],3],["x59",0,6,"What does an active survey measure that a passive survey does not?",["Beacon signal strength from each AP","The noise floor on each channel","The list of nearby SSIDs","Associated client performance, such as throughput"],3,"An active survey associates to the network and measures throughput, retries, loss and roaming. Beacon signal strength is collected by passive surveys too.","Domain 6: Site survey types",["Passive surveys also record beacon signal from every AP they hear.","Noise floor is measured in passive surveys and spectrum analysis, not only active surveys.","Nearby SSIDs are listed by any passive scan.",null],1],["x60",0,6,"Clients on a new SSID receive IP addresses from the wrong subnet. What should be checked first?",["The antenna gain on the AP","SSID-to-VLAN mapping and trunk VLANs","The AP's DTIM interval","The RADIUS certificate's expiry date"],1,"A wrong subnet means the SSID is bridged to the wrong VLAN or the switch trunk is not carrying the right one. A RADIUS certificate problem would stop authentication, not hand out the wrong subnet.","Domain 6: Connection problems",["Antenna gain changes RF coverage, not which subnet DHCP assigns.",null,"DTIM affects power save timing and has nothing to do with IP addressing.","An expired RADIUS certificate would stop authentication rather than give out the wrong subnet."],2]]);
